text:
Learning Outcomes
By the end of these notes you can:

name the three cases where a team’s judgement of its own game reliably fails;
distinguish telemetry, performance capture and asking people, and say what each can and cannot tell you;
rewrite a vague design question as a measure you could record for a single player;
write down, before collecting anything, the result that would change your mind;
load a playtest CSV into R and take a first look at it with read_csv(), glimpse() and summary().

Learning Outcomes
By the end of these notes you can:

explain why an average on its own cannot describe a playtest;
read a standard deviation as the typical distance of a player from the mean;
describe a distribution by its centre, spread, skew and outliers;
say whether an outlier is an error, an extreme player or the finding itself;
read the mean, median, standard deviation and high percentiles together and say what each adds;
draw hist(), boxplot(), barplot() and plot() in base R and say what each one is for.

Learning Outcomes
By the end of these notes you can:

classify a variable as nominal, ordinal, discrete or continuous, and say which operations that allows;
recognise a tidy table and name the most common way data fails to be one;
say what a sample is, and why every number you compute describes the sample rather than your audience;
choose between mean and median, and read the gap between them as evidence of skew;
describe spread with the standard deviation, quartiles and IQR, and say why the range is rarely enough;
draw a histogram and a boxplot in base R and say what each shows that a summary hides.
